  "source": "Run 1 original final three messages, mechanical excerpts only",
  "threatProse": "Tam answered with a click of his tongue and a chirrup to the oxen, and the yard gate swung shut behind the cart.\n\nThe low stretch past the west gate was as bad as the landlady had promised \u2014 Thursday's rain still stood in the bowl of the road, and the near wheel went in to the hub at the worst of it. They lost the better part of an hour levering it out with the spare stave, Tam swearing steadily at the oxen, who ignored him, and the morning was half gone before the road firmed.\n\nAfter that the river road ran easy. Hedgerows, a roadside shrine with fresh-cut flowers, woodsmoke off a hamlet at midday. Tam drove and watched and said nothing worth saying; he ate his own sausage one-handed at a walk and let the silence sit. Past midday the land began tilting toward the water, and late in the afternoon the river showed itself \u2014 broad and brown-green, running quick, with the Reedford landing plain on the south bank: a crane, drying racks strung with dyed cloth, a walled yard. Between it and the water lay the ford, marked with stakes, and along the south bank the willow scrub came down thick and green right to the edge of the gravel.\n\nThe sun was leaning on the western hills. Tam looked at it, then at the scrub, and clicked the oxen up. \"Would've beat this by an hour, without the wet road. Sit tight.\"\n\nThe cart took the water. Cold rolled off the river, the wheels found the stony shelf, and the oxen leaned into the current with their ears back. Halfway across, water white at the axles \u2014\n\nThe scrub moved.\n\nFive men rose out of it on the south bank, four with bows already at half-draw, arrowheads sloped toward the cart. The fifth stood out ahead of them at the gravel's edge, a long knife held loose in one hand, and called across the water in an easy, carrying voice.\n\n\"That's far enough. Walk her in slow and tip the vats onto the bank \u2014 drivers keep their teeth, every time, so long as the dye comes out.\"\n\nTam hauled back on the reins and the oxen plashed to a halt, the cart sitting broadside in the current. \"Hold,\" he said, low, without turning his head.",
